 'An environmental historian who works across large geographical and chronological scales. His presidential address treats environmental change as a problem that historical inquiry can investigate with expanding bodies of evidence. The earlier checked passages of “Observations on the Nature and Culture of Environmental History” place the field among several traditions while keeping its relation to the Annales qualified. Something New Under the Sun studies twentieth-century environmental change; Mosquito Empires brings ecology and politics together in the Greater Caribbean. These book descriptions remain distinct from the primary passages read. The existing environmental and global roster contexts remain in place.',
 'Something New Under the Sun (2000); “Observations on the Nature and Culture of Environmental History” (2003); Mosquito Empires: Ecology and War in the Greater Caribbean, 1620–1914 (2010).',
